**1. Analysis & Description of the Attached PDF:**

The provided PDF consists of two research papers published in 2015 (Journal of Controlled Release) and 2017 (npj Vaccines), both focusing on "Nano-11," a dendrimer-like alpha-D-glucan nanoparticle, as a vaccine adjuvant. The studies demonstrate that Nano-11, derived from sweet corn and possessing a positive surface charge, efficiently delivers antigens to dendritic cells (DCs) and enhances their activation. It elicits a robust antibody response comparable to or superior to aluminum adjuvants, while inducing a transient, localized inflammatory response primarily involving monocytes and macrophages. Nano-11 targets migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes, showing promise as a safe, effective, and cost-effective next-generation vaccine adjuvant.

**2. Independent Analysis & Description of the Image:**

The image displays a Western blot analysis, separated into "Supernatant" and "Cell lysate" fractions under four distinct experimental conditions: untreated, LPS-stimulated, Nano-11 treated, and LPS plus Nano-11 treated. Molecular weight markers are indicated at 30kD, 20kD, and 15kD. In the cell lysate, a protein band is prominently observed around 30kD in LPS-treated samples. In contrast, the supernatant shows a strong protein band around 17kD in Nano-11 treated samples, which is further enhanced when combined with LPS. This pattern suggests detection of a protein that undergoes cleavage from a higher molecular weight precursor (in lysate) to a lower molecular weight active form (in supernatant) under specific stimulation conditions.

**3. Relevance of the Image to the Scientific Topic of the PDF:**

The image is highly relevant to the scientific topic of the PDF, as it is Figure 7D from the 2015 Journal of Controlled Release paper. It provides crucial evidence for the mechanism by which Nano-11 activates dendritic cells. The PDF states that Nano-11 induces the secretion of IL-1β, a key pro-inflammatory cytokine, and mentions that ELISA does not differentiate between inactive (31kD) and active (17kD) forms. This Western blot directly addresses that by showing that LPS stimulation leads to pro-IL-1β (approximately 30kD) in cell lysates, while Nano-11 (especially with LPS priming) triggers the processing and secretion of the active, mature IL-1β (approximately 17kD) into the supernatant. This visually confirms Nano-11's role in activating the inflammasome and producing active IL-1β.